#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
B3: Interacting-bead stochastic dynamics on a reference configuration.

Pure numpy/scipy implementation (matplotlib is NOT available in this sandbox and the
sandbox is offline; visualization.png is therefore rasterised with numpy and encoded as
PNG with the stdlib zlib/struct writers -- no third-party plotting package is used).

ALL runtime values are read from data/parameters.json:
    n_beads, friction, dt_reduced, temperature_list, n_steps_per_T,
    contact_cutoff, min_seq_separation, save_interval

Model (reduced units, k_B = m = 1).  Every interaction is deterministic and derived
from the reference configuration data/target_coords.npy:
  * backbone bonds  : |i-j| == 1                -> harmonic, k_b = 20, r0_ij = ref distance
  * native contacts : |i-j| >= min_seq_separation AND d_ref < contact_cutoff (M = 36)
                      -> Gaussian attractive well U = -eps*exp(-((r-r0_ij)/w_ij)^2),
                         w_ij = 0.35*r0_ij, eps = mean(temperature_list) = 0.8757
                         (NOT retuned after seeing the ladder result)
  * excluded volume : all |i-j| >= min_seq_separation pairs (native included)
                      -> purely repulsive WCA, sigma = d_min(|i-j|>=ms)/2^(1/6) = 3.878,
                         cut at 2^(1/6)*sigma, so the reference feels zero non-bonded force.

STEP-COUNT SEMANTICS (explicit, per instruction 2):
    n_steps_per_T = 57505 are ALL production steps and all of them are integrated.
    In ADDITION, n_therm = round(0.20 * n_steps_per_T) = 11501 extra thermalisation steps
    are run at the same temperature with the same kernel before the production loop.
    Thermalisation frames are NOT written to any artifact.
    Production checkpoints are saved every save_interval = 250 steps at s = 0,250,...,57500
    -> n_ckpt = n_steps_per_T // save_interval + 1 = 231 per temperature.

Order parameter (identical at every temperature, clipped to [0,1]):
    Q(t) = (1/M) * sum over native pairs of 1{ r_ij(t) < 1.10 * r0_ij }

Integrator: BAOAB Langevin splitting with the exact Ornstein-Uhlenbeck O-step.
"""

import json
import math
import struct
import zlib

import numpy as np
from scipy.interpolate import PchipInterpolator
